The GACL package provides CMake targets:

    find_package(GACL CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE GACL::gacl_lib)

Public headers are installed to include/gacl/:
    #include <gacl/gacl.h>
    #include <gacl/shuffle.h>

Preprocessor definitions provided by the package:
    GACL_INCLUDE_BLER=1  (Block-Level Entropy Reduction)
    GACL_INCLUDE_CLER=1  (Component-Level Entropy Reduction — enabled by default)

Runtime dependency (cler feature):
    This port includes onnxruntime.dll and onnxruntime_providers_shared.dll
    (Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime 1.24.2). Both must sit next to your executable
    at runtime.

    Building with the vcpkg toolchain copies onnxruntime.dll next to your
    executable automatically. It does NOT copy
    onnxruntime_providers_shared.dll, which has no CMake target and must be
    named directly.

    To deploy both, including for `cmake --install`:

        include(GNUInstallDirs)
        if(TARGET OnnxRuntime::OnnxRuntime)
            get_target_property(_ort_dll OnnxRuntime::OnnxRuntime IMPORTED_LOCATION)
            get_filename_component(_ort_bin "${_ort_dll}" DIRECTORY)
            install(FILES
                "${_ort_bin}/onnxruntime.dll"
                "${_ort_bin}/onnxruntime_providers_shared.dll"
                DESTINATION "${CMAKE_INSTALL_BINDIR}")
        endif()

    The DLLs are also in <your build dir>/vcpkg_installed/<triplet>/bin/ if
    you prefer to copy them yourself.

To build without CLER (and drop the OnnxRuntime dependency), declare the
dependency in your manifest with default features off:

    { "name": "microsoft-gacl", "default-features": false }

    (classic mode: vcpkg install microsoft-gacl[core])

CLER is enabled by default. The ONNX models it needs are NOT shipped in the
package - they are about 65 MB and you generate them once:

    pwsh <your build dir>/vcpkg_installed/<triplet>/tools/microsoft-gacl/setupCLER.ps1 `
         -ModelsDir "C:\<your project>\ThirdParty\models"

Then point your build at them, or GACL runs without CLER:

    cmake -B build -DGACL_MODELS_DIR="C:\<your project>\ThirdParty\models"

Generating the models needs Python 3.12 and uv, and downloads about 2 GB of
PyTorch. No GPU is required - the export runs on CPU.

Compressing NON-TEXTURE data (important):

    GACL's hardware-accelerated decompression path requires a zstd window of
    at most 256 KB (windowLog 18). Data compressed with a larger window still
    decompresses correctly, but falls off the hardware path and decodes
    roughly 100x slower. It is a performance cliff, not a corruption bug -
    nothing fails loudly if you get it wrong.

    If you compress with stock zstd.exe instead of using the GACL APIs or
    front end tool, set the window explicitly:

        zstd --zstd=wlog=18 <input>

    Two things to know about that flag:
      - It is not documented upstream. You will not find it in `zstd --help`
        or in the manual; it works regardless.
      - Do NOT reach for --long= instead. It exists to make windows LARGER,
        and setting it low also changes other strategy parameters. It is not
        a way to spell wlog=18.

    If you LINK libzstd, a command-line flag cannot reach your in-process
    compression context. Call GACL_Compression_CompressBuffer() instead, or
    GACL_ShuffleCompress_BCn() for texture data.

    If you need the ZSTD_CCtx itself - for streaming, dictionaries, or a
    reused context - GACL_Compression_DefaultInitRoutine() hands back one
    already configured to the settings above, and
    GACL_Compression_DefaultCleanupRoutine() frees it.

    The emitted frame declares a window of AT MOST 256 KB - zstd reduces it
    further for small inputs, and a decoder provisioned for 256 KB accepts any
    frame declaring 256 KB or less.
